-**Build system:** `.gitattributes` pins LF at checkout repo-wide (`* text=auto eol=lf`, added 2026-09-13), and beneath it keeps `coverage-matrix.md text eol=lf` (added 2026-09-06) as the NAMED gate-correctness control, not cosmetics: the coverage-completeness gate byte-compares the committed `coverage-matrix.md` against `CoverageMatrix::render()`, which is `writeln!`-built (LF) and written verbatim, so the equality holds only where checkout preserves LF — `core.autocrlf=true` is the norm on the Windows dev host and the one CI job, `rust`, runs on a `windows-latest` runner (the `a11y` job's pinned `windows-2022` image and its msedgedriver pin gate left with the window on 2026-10-11; their text is last at `321dc8f`). The wildcard IS the repo-wide policy; the named rule keeps that one path's guarantee independent of any later narrowing of it. The pin renormalizes nothing — `git ls-files --eol` measured 3031 `i/lf` · 13 `i/none` · 4 `i/-text`, no `i/crlf` and no `i/mixed`, against a system-gitconfig `core.autocrlf=true`, and git's "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warning went from firing on every touched file to 0 (as measured 2026-09-13 at `2026-09-13-audit-debt-retired-before-epoch-1-closes`). Cargo (cargo 1.85, Rust 2024 edition, MSRV 1.94.1). Lint with `cargo clippy`; test with `cargo nextest` — the pinned runner, driven through a zero-retry `ci` profile in `.config/nextest.toml` (no retries mask flakes — the determinism invariant) plus `cargo test --doc` for the doctests nextest skips — over the dev-test stack rstest/proptest/insta/assert_cmd/assert_fs/predicates/regex/sha2 (`regex` a `conductor-core` dev-dependency edge for the secret-scan and workflow env-context gates, locked transitively; `sha2` a `conductor-run` one for the real-model harvest's digest pins, which since 2026-10-11 locks it itself); line coverage via `cargo-llvm-cov` (requires the `llvm-tools-preview` toolchain component). Exact tool/dep versions live in test-plan §4. `bundled` SQLite compiles SQLite C from source (longer cold builds, fully self-contained). tonic 0.14 moved prost codegen to `tonic-prost-build` — build scripts use that crate (budget a small adjustment when mining Pulse's prior-art injectors that predate the split). Optional `cargo-modules` / `cargo-rail` audit the dependency graph. No build step precedes `cargo build` since 2026-10-11: the `conductor-tauri` GUI bin, its `tauri-build` frontend-bundle prerequisite and the harness shells' `ensure_frontend` step left with the window. `deny.toml` ignores no advisory and allows 8 licenses since then, when the Tauri tree's 16 `unmaintained` ignores and the `MPL-2.0` allow left with their subject; `Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception` stays for its current holders (`blake3`, `rustix`/`linux-raw-sys`, the `wasi`/`wit-bindgen`/`wasm-*` tree), and `BSD-2-Clause` covers `arrayref` (via `conductor-emit`→`blake3`). That license policy also covers the workspace's OWN `conductor-*` crates — `deny.toml` carries no `private` exemption — and each passes as `MIT OR Apache-2.0`, inherited from `[workspace.package]` (`license.workspace = true` in every member), so a member without an allowed license fails `cargo deny check licenses`; `publish = false` exempts nothing. Each entry names its crate and dependency path inline; BOTH supply-chain runners are green over the un-drifted lock — `cargo deny check advisories bans licenses sources` exit 0 and `cargo audit` exit 0 (1296 advisories loaded, 268 crates scanned, no allowed warning, as measured at the `2026-10-11-conductors-window-retired` gate over a porcelain-clean advisory-db copy; the 2026-09-05 reading — 1239 advisories, 562 packages, 17 allowed warnings — stands as that date's record). The long-standing red of 2026-08 was a LOCAL host-checkout artifact, never an external advisory-DB fault: an untracked `crates/gettext-sys/RUSTSEC-2026-0244.md` left in this host's advisory-db clone after upstream moved that file to `crates/gettext-rs/` on 2026-08-09 (`e12b689b`), which a fetch into the existing copy never removes — so the checkout's `status --porcelain` is read BEFORE any parse failure is classified external (security-plan §Dependency Security).
